WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.667
SEAN WELLECK: Today,
I'm excited to talk

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:04.250
about bridging informal and
formal mathematical reasoning

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.005
with AI.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.640
So probably throughout
this course,

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.280
you've seen many
different ways where

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.120
these AI agents can start
to have a lot of impact

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.590
in different areas.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:22.640
And so one interesting
question is to what extent

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.990
can they impact
more expert domains,

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.440
so, say, finance,
science, medicine,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.540
and the domain that
I'll talk about today,

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.200
which is mathematics.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.220
Now, with mathematics,
there's a variety of things

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.430
that you could imagine doing
with an agent or a language

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:41.220
model.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.850
You could try to have
an open-ended dialogue

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:44.790
with the model.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.030
You could try to have it come
up with counterexamples for you,

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.110
help you write proofs.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:55.100
Your imagination is
basically the limit.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.780
And what I want
to argue today is

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.940
that for math in
particular, how you

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.000
represent the math is actually
extremely important to think

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.470
about.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.590
So one way to
represent math is what

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.940
is called informal mathematics.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.480
Here, math is
represented as raw data.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.860
So it could be text.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.270
It could be an image.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.810
It could be even audio,
if you're talking

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.550
with someone about math.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.890
And doing this is
extremely flexible.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.750
And in some sense, most of the
math that you've encountered

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:33.100
or that you've used is probably
in some kind of informal format.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.910
So even something like a latex
solution or an archive paper,

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.900
we would consider
to be informal.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.070
Now, when we think about using
AI to generate or operate

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:49.540
in informal math, there is
a really important problem,

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.190
which is that we don't have
any guarantees that the math is

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.720
correct or not.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.700
So here is an
example of a language

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.410
model producing a solution.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.900
And the answer at the
end is actually correct.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:08.110
But makes these subtle mistakes
in the intermediate steps.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.690
And so for really
simple math, this

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.170
might not actually be an issue.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.800
And the language model
might still be useful.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.500
But if we really want to
trust it in, say, education,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.130
or in research
level math, then it

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:25.720
could get very tedious
and very difficult

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.040
to know whether the
outputs are correct or not.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.850
So this motivates the other
end of the spectrum, which

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:35.630
is called formal mathematics.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.910
Here, math is written as source
code in a programming language.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.250
And basically, the
way it works is

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:46.420
you can write a specification
or a lemma or theorem, like,

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.470
1 plus 1 equals 2, then
you can write a proof.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.860
And the really cool thing
is that if all of the code

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:58.580
compiles, then you have a formal
guarantee that the proof is

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:00.770
correct.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.620
And so this is a lot different
than the informal math

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.690
because now we
can have certainty

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:09.350
if, say, a language model
generates a proof, that it

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:15.110
is actually a reliable and
correct proof of the theorem.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.750
And there's been a variety
of these theorem proving

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.560
languages developed.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.530
I'll mostly focus on a
language called Lean,

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:27.830
which recently is having
a really exciting growth

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:31.770
and really exciting intersection
with the mathematical community,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.500
which I'll talk about in a bit.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.870
And for one of the topics
that I'll talk about,

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:40.050
I'll also mention this
language called Isabelle

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.320
because it has
some features that

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.810
make it kind of
amenable to writing

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:49.190
different types of
proofs with AI systems.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.190
So if you haven't seen
one of these before,

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:55.740
I wanted to actually show you
what one of these formal proof

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.960
assistants looked like.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.440
And so to do this, we'll try
to prove this really simple

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.420
theorem, which is transitivity.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.140
So if R is a subset of S
and S is a subset of T,

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.670
then R is a subset of T.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:16.300
So I'll actually show
you this as a demo.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:20.760
And so here what I've done
is I've opened up VS Code.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.650
And so as I mentioned, this
is literally like programming,

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.280
like writing code.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.930
And here you could see
the theorem statement.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.570
So if R is a subset of S
and S is a subset of T,

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:39.360
then R is a subset of
T. And these are often

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.090
called interactive
proof assistants

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.200
because what happens is,
as I go to write a proof,

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.440
it actually tells me
over here, what is

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.630
the current state of the proof.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:55.460
So the idea isn't to learn
every bit of the formal system.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:58.180
But I'm just showing you
the important parts that

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.980
will be relevant for the talk.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.840
So here we can see these
different variables.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:07.930
And we can see what we have
remaining to prove over here.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:11.200
Now the thing is that it takes
some expertise to actually write

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.210
a proof in Lean.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:17.140
So for example, if I know some
Lean, then one thing I can do

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:20.140
is this command here.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.000
And you can notice
that it actually

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.690
changed the state of the proof.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.260
So now I have these
two variables up here.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.190
And the goal has also changed.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.890
Then again, if you
have some expertise,

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:37.180
then you can actually know
that this idea of transitivity

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.690
has already been
programmed into Lean.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:47.410
So here I wrote this
transitivity rule here or lemma.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:49.450
And now you can see
up here that it says

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:51.020
that there's no goals left.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.220
So I've completed the proof.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.350
So we'll actually touch
on a lot of things

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:58.670
that I've talked about
here throughout the talk.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:02.410
We'll talk about the fact that
it requires some expertise

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:06.220
to actually write a proof in
one of these proof assistants.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.440
And if you look
at this proof, it

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:11.050
might look a bit
different than one

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:14.020
that you would see
written down in a textbook

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:15.370
or something like that.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:20.050
So we'll revisit these
ideas throughout the talk.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:25.540
So now, going back
to the talk, we

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:28.640
could ask, like, OK, I showed
you this very simple example.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.680
But is this toy or is
this actually useful

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:35.770
for more complex mathematics?

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:39.370
So a really exciting thing is
that, increasingly, there's

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:43.450
a larger and larger intersection
with the mathematical community.

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:47.540
So here's an example of the
mathematician Terence Tao.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:52.640
And he formalized, in Lean,
the proof of a theorem from one

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.820
of his actual papers.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:59.914
And a really exciting thing
that's playing into this

00:06:59.914 --> 00:07:02.990
is this project, which
is called Mathlib.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:07.220
So if you noticed, in the demo I
gave, this idea of transitivity

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.470
was already kind
of programmed in.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.460
And that's the idea of Mathlib.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.440
So lots of people are
contributing and building up

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.390
foundations that
other people can

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:25.340
use to make it easier to write
more and more complex math.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.720
And if you look at what
mathematicians write or talk

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:32.580
to them and listen
to what they say,

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.550
then these formal
proof assistants

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.250
are exciting for a
few different reasons.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:40.670
So one is that they
can potentially enable

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:43.100
a new form of collaboration.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:45.380
So what you can do
is you can break down

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:51.510
a big problem or big proof
into multiple pieces.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:54.150
And then anyone can
go on and essentially

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:57.015
submit code to try to
solve one of the pieces

00:07:57.015 --> 00:08:02.820
to, say, prove one of the helper
theorems or helper lemmas.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.850
And then the cool thing
is that we know for a fact

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:10.050
that we can trust the code
since Lean will automatically

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:11.730
check it.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.670
And so this is a lot
different than the typical way

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:18.220
of collaborating on,
say, a math paper,

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:20.010
where you have to
trust the author

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:23.820
or read through their results.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:27.250
It also gives you instant
feedback, as we saw.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:30.450
So this could be useful in
a variety of ways, including

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:32.940
when trying to learn math.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:38.100
And, of course, it has these
guarantees on correctness.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:39.780
So, hopefully,
I've convinced you

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.270
that formal math is
getting more and more

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:46.530
interesting for learning or
for the actual mathematical

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:47.500
community.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:50.650
But why is it actually
important for AI?

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.450
Well, one thing is
that, as we all know,

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:57.520
we are not able to
automatically check or trust

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.520
the outputs of AI models.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.620
And so what the
formal system gives us

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:07.130
is some way of just
preventing incorrect math.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:10.150
Or it turns out, you can
actually use this for code

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:12.160
as well.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:15.580
Increasingly, we see more
and more models trained

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:17.360
with reinforcement learning.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.020
And I'll actually talk about
one of those in the talk.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:23.830
And what you can think of
the formal system as being is

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:26.200
it gives you this
perfect reward signal

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:29.472
of whether something is
correct or not correct.

00:09:29.472 --> 00:09:32.560
And this turns out to
be very useful for then

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.220
improving machine
learning models further.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.060
It also gets at one of the
hot topics in the field

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.260
right now, which is reasoning.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:43.990
And it gives you
this nice playground

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:48.170
where you can try even very
easy reasoning problems to,

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:54.350
essentially, arbitrarily, hard
ones within the domain of math.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:57.710
And so if we look at the
history of language models

00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:01.550
in formal math, one of
the pioneering works

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:05.900
happened in 2020 from these
two authors from OpenAI.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:09.300
And they developed a
system called gpt-f.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.750
And so it actually
used a language model

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:16.010
to predict the next
steps of these proofs.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:19.770
And even back then, they got
some pretty interesting results.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:23.760
So what they did is they had
the system generate a proof,

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.450
and then they submitted
it as a pull request.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:29.960
And here are some of the
comments from the maintainers

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:33.320
of the formal proof library.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:36.780
Even this model, back then,
could produce shorter proofs.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:40.880
And some of them were even
philosophically more concise

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:43.700
than the human-written ones.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.280
And then since then,
we've seen rapid progress

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:50.070
in using methods based
on language models

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.410
for formal theorem proving.

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.960
So here is showing
the performance

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:57.430
on a really common benchmark.

00:10:57.430 --> 00:10:59.980
These are based on math
competition problems.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.590
And I actually made this
plot a few months ago.

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.793
But it's already out of date.

00:11:04.793 --> 00:11:06.210
Actually, just
this morning, there

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:12.660
was a model released that
gets 80% on this benchmark.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.595
And just to give you some
flavor of what these look like,

00:11:15.595 --> 00:11:17.970
again, you don't have to read
through the details of this

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:21.540
or through most of the
examples I show in this talk.

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:26.910
But here's an actual output from
a model called DeepSeek Prover.

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:32.280
And it managed to produce
this proof of an IMO problem.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:34.140
And so, hopefully, it
gives you some sense

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:36.900
that the outputs of
these are starting

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:40.560
to get pretty non-trivial.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:44.200
And Terence Tao, if
we go back to members

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.840
of the mathematical
community, wrote

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:48.830
that during his
formalization project,

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:51.470
he was actually
using GitHub Copilot,

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:56.630
and sometimes it was able to
infer the directions he was

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:00.250
going in and things like that.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:03.760
So with all this, with
formal math being so exciting

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:08.300
for both mathematics and
for AI, the question is,

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.870
why don't people in AI
always use formal math?

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:15.760
What is the blocker
to making this happen?

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:20.350
So I want to argue that the
key challenge is that there's

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:26.140
this gap between how people
are familiar with doing math

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:28.900
and the actual formal system.

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:33.740
In particular, there's just
so many ideas and intuitions,

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:36.130
and even the way
that we write proofs,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:40.120
that are just difficult
or very tedious to express

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:42.020
in the formal system.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:45.650
In a formal system like
Lean, each step of reasoning

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:49.220
needs to be explicitly
spelled out in detail.

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:52.770
And as we saw even in that very
simple example that I gave,

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:56.040
you often have to have a
deep knowledge of the system,

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.680
like what things
exist in the system,

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.490
and how to write
the code, and so on.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.380
And so the theme
of this talk will

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:08.510
be steps towards bridging the
gap between informal reasoning

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:10.760
and formal reasoning.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:13.580
Can we use AI and
language models

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:16.820
to get the best of
both worlds, maintain

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.950
the flexibility of
informal reasoning

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:20.810
while having all
of these benefits

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:25.670
that I mentioned with
operating in the formal system?

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:28.520
And so I'll talk about
three different directions

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:32.600
that we and others in the
community are pursuing.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:36.260
First, I'll talk about
incorporating informal thoughts

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:39.260
into the formal theorem
proving procedure.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:43.460
I'll talk about training
models to think informally

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:47.670
with reinforcement learning, and
in particular, our recent work,

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:50.720
which is called Lean-STaR.

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:56.160
Then I'll move to informal
provers or informal proofs.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.610
And I'll talk about
how you can break down

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:01.490
the problem of writing
one of these proofs

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:05.900
as first writing a sketch, and
then filling in the lower level

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:07.850
gaps of the sketch.

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:12.020
So I'll talk about our work
called Draft-Sketch-Prove,

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:15.440
and then our recent work, which
is actually not even released

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.950
yet, of trying to
enable tools that

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:22.010
let you fill in the gaps of
these sketches in the Lean

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:24.290
Theorem Prover.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.310
And then finally, at
the end of the talk,

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:30.200
I will talk about more
of a future direction

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.730
that we are taking
initial steps towards,

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:34.760
which is, how do
we actually make

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:37.910
these systems useful
to mathematicians

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:40.170
doing research
level mathematics.

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:43.020
So in particular, I'll
talk about one way

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:47.040
in which the models could
assist in these larger projects.

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:49.690
I'll talk about some tools
that actually already exist.

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:52.260
And you can try, after
the talk, if you want,

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:58.260
and a new benchmark that tries
to further progress of building

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:01.710
these assistants for these
complex, research level type

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.380
projects.

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.480
So with that, let me
go to the first part

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:10.110
where I will talk about
integrating informal thoughts

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:13.140
into formal theorem proving.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.030
So here, this will
largely be based

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:18.570
on our paper called
Lean-STaR, which we'll

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:21.930
be presenting soon at ICLR.

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:24.120
And here, again,
we're in the setting

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:27.900
of theorem proving in Lean.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:31.980
So here we have the theorem that
I showed you before at the top.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.060
And then as I showed
you in the demo,

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:35.550
you could think
of this as a kind

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.410
of interactive
step-by-step process.

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:42.490
So in particular, Lean will give
you this state of the proof.

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:44.500
And then one thing
that you could do

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:48.410
is you could send that
state to a language model,

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:52.060
and it could try to generate
the next step of the proof.

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:55.690
Then you can provide
the next step to Lean.

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:59.330
And it can either tell you
whether the proof is done,

00:15:59.330 --> 00:16:02.150
whether there is an
error, or, as we saw it,

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:04.750
it can give you the
next state of the proof.

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:08.500
You could then continue this
until you either give up

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:11.380
or you reach a complete proof.

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:14.080
And so in that sense, you
can see one of these proofs

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:17.570
as being a sequence of
states and next steps,

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:18.653
or they're called tactics.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:25.090
So in a bit more detail,
what we want to do

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:30.820
is train a model on some data
set of inputs and outputs.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:33.910
So an example is you could have
the model take in the proof

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:36.590
state and output the next step.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:39.740
And what you typically
do is you extract

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:42.410
a lot of these state
and next step examples

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:46.130
from some corpus of
theorems and proofs.

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:49.700
So for example, you could use
the entire Mathlib project

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:51.420
that I mentioned earlier.

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:54.470
Or you could even have a model
generate its own theorems

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:56.100
and generate its own proofs.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:17:00.860
And then, ultimately, you
turn it into a data set.

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:03.290
Once you've trained
a model, you then

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:06.109
want to use it to
generate full proofs.

00:17:06.109 --> 00:17:08.510
So the traditional
way of doing this

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:11.690
is through this tree
search procedure.

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:15.290
Here's an example tree search
called best-first search.

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.800
And basically, the idea
is you ask the model

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:22.730
to generate multiple
candidates for the next step.

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:25.430
Then you somehow
come up with a score

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:27.740
for each one of
these candidates.

00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:31.230
You choose the step
with the highest score.

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:37.020
So for instance, first, we
chose this intro h step.

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:39.690
Then you can expand
those further.

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:41.860
Choose the next highest
scoring sequence.

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:44.080
As you can see here,
it kind of backtracked.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:45.960
And then it took
this green path,

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:49.620
which led to a correct proof.

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:51.720
So this is just one
possible strategy

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:56.220
you could use for generating
a full proof if your model can

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:59.730
only predict the next step.

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:03.180
So what we are
interested in is, can we

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:07.290
go beyond just training
the model on formal proofs,

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:10.500
and to generate formal
proofs, and instead

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:14.940
train the model to think before
each step of the formal proof?

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.160
So in particular, we
want to train the model

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:21.340
to generate what's on the right,
where before taking a step,

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:23.550
it outputs some reasoning.

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.490
And it's free to
decide whatever it

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:27.880
wants to do with the reasoning.

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:29.830
It could break down the problem.

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:31.480
It could plan out the next step.

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:34.010
It could even solve
some computation

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:37.880
that isn't really present
in the formal proof.

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:39.810
So why would you
want to do this?

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:42.170
Well, again, it
could give the model

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:46.430
some room to plan out
what it is about to do.

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:50.640
When you think about it, it can
help diversify the search space,

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:54.170
since now the model is
searching for proofs not

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:58.190
just in the formal space, but
in the formal space augmented

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:00.200
with these thoughts.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.130
And I won't go
into details here.

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:05.360
But there's been a lot of cool
work in the language model

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:09.950
community showing that adding
in these chain of thought

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:13.730
in other domains can
actually give a model which

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:17.330
is more expressive in terms
of the kinds of problems

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:19.340
it can solve.

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:23.360
So we have a few good
reasons to try this out.

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:27.650
Now, the key issue here is
that nowhere on the internet

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:30.800
is there formal
proofs written in Lean

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:34.400
that have these nice thoughts
written out for them.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:38.840
And so what we have to do is
actually design an algorithm so

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:42.860
that a model learns on its own
to produce these thoughts that

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:46.070
are useful for the proofs.

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:49.050
So the algorithm is
called Lean-STaR.

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.890
And it adopts its name
from this other work

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:54.050
called self-taught reasoner.

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:57.740
And the process basically
has two different phases.

00:19:57.740 --> 00:19:59.570
So the high-level
idea is we want

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:02.540
to learn to generate these
thoughts via reinforcement

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:03.680
learning.

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:07.370
So first, we have to initialize
the model so that it can

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:09.470
produce some type of thoughts.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:12.800
And then we want to train it
further so that it gets better

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:16.700
at using the thoughts
to prove theorems.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:20.790
So in the first step we
use this clever trick,

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:24.560
which is we can look at a
state and the actual next step

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:28.830
of the proof, and then
have a language model just

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:32.940
retrospectively come up
with some thought that

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:36.870
describes the transition from
the state to the next step.

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:40.300
So what we found is that if
you don't give this step,

00:20:40.300 --> 00:20:42.420
then the language
model might just

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:45.600
output a meaningless
or incorrect thought.

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.780
But they were good
enough at coming up

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:54.180
with some thought which
described this transition.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:59.230
So then we can annotate
a large set of proofs.

00:20:59.230 --> 00:21:02.190
So here, for instance,
we take all of Mathlib,

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.710
and we can get
hundreds of thousands

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:10.050
of examples of state
thought and next step.

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:13.330
Then we can use that
to train a model.

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:17.470
And it doesn't necessarily have
to use all 100,000, by the way,

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:18.810
for this initial step.

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:21.660
You want to just train
some initial model that

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:28.210
knows to produce these
thoughts prior to taking steps.

00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:31.720
Then we want to take that model
and put it into this loop.

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:35.410
So we have the model
generate full proofs.

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:40.030
Then we ask Lean to tell us
whether they are correct or not.

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:43.390
And then we use some type of
reinforcement learning algorithm

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:46.510
to improve the model further.

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:50.060
So what we need is some
method to generate proofs.

00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:52.390
And we need to actually
choose some algorithm

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:55.210
to implement this with.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:59.680
So what we discovered is
that this commonly used way

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:02.480
of using the tree search,
or best first search,

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:04.660
was actually difficult
to get to work when

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:06.850
we added in these thoughts.

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:08.680
I think one reason
is that it gets

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:11.080
difficult to actually
assign a really

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:13.660
good score to the thought.

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:20.350
And so as a result, the tree
search didn't work as well.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:23.360
So what we did is
design a new method,

00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:26.990
which is this simple
idea of generating

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:29.150
multiple proofs in parallel.

00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:33.930
So for a given trajectory,
you generate a step.

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:36.140
If you get an error
from Lean, you just

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:38.130
retry on that same path.

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:41.880
And then you keep going up
to a maximum number of steps,

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:45.535
or you stop if you
reach a complete proof.

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:53.090
Then for the algorithm, we
actually just used the simplest

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:54.830
possible algorithm.

00:22:54.830 --> 00:22:56.810
And so we, as well
as other groups,

00:22:56.810 --> 00:23:01.040
are now exploring
better reinforcement

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:04.310
learning algorithms,
like online algorithms.

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:06.510
But here we did
something very simple,

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:10.740
which is you just collect
the successful trajectories.

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:13.070
You add those to your data set.

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:17.670
And then you train a new model
on this data set of states,

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:19.700
thoughts, and tactics.

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:22.400
So this algorithm
goes by various names.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.600
It's called expert iteration.

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:26.610
It's also called ReST-EM.

00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:29.160
It's also called
rejection fine-tuning.

00:23:29.160 --> 00:23:31.353
But the idea is very simple.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:37.280
So then, typically, the way
that these models are evaluated

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:40.940
is on these math
competition problems.

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:44.960
So although we train
the model on Mathlib,

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:47.940
here's a common test
data set which is used,

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:49.820
which is called miniF2F.

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:52.880
And here's an example problem
from the International Math

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:54.170
Olympiad.

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:57.150
So here I've shown
the informal version,

00:23:57.150 --> 00:24:00.210
just to show you what
the problem is like.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:04.010
But then the model here
only gets the formal version

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:05.485
and has to produce a proof.

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:11.660
So here are some results
with an early model

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:14.270
that we tried in the project.

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:18.600
So in red, you can see the
model that we started with.

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:20.570
And then in black, you
could see the model

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:24.480
after we perform this
initialization step.

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.310
Then in gray, we
can see the model

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:29.220
after we perform the
expert iteration.

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:31.950
And you can see that the
Lean-STaR process basically took

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:35.970
it from being worse than
this baseline model, which

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:42.120
was based on this GPT-4 agent,
to being better than it.

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:45.060
Then at the end
of the project, we

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:48.480
applied Lean-STaR to the
best available base model

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:51.210
at the time for theorem proving.

00:24:51.210 --> 00:24:53.730
And again, we saw
these improvements

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:55.830
going from gray to black.

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:57.915
And at the time
that we did this,

00:24:57.915 --> 00:25:01.500
this was the state-of-the-art
for this miniF2F data set.

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:07.350
One really interesting
thing is that we

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:10.410
found that models
with the thoughts

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:14.170
benefit more from scaling
up the search budget,

00:25:14.170 --> 00:25:16.320
or the inference budget.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:20.500
So here we're seeing, as we
sample more and more steps,

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:23.530
either with our sampling
method, or as a baseline

00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:28.750
with the best first search,
how the performance looks.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:31.880
And you can see that the gains
we get from the green line,

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:34.130
which is Lean-STaR
with the thoughts,

00:25:34.130 --> 00:25:37.390
are more pronounced
than the model that

00:25:37.390 --> 00:25:39.790
doesn't have the thoughts.

00:25:39.790 --> 00:25:42.520
And so this might be
indicating the idea

00:25:42.520 --> 00:25:45.190
that I mentioned before,
that adding in these thoughts

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:49.930
helps to diversify
the search space.

00:25:49.930 --> 00:25:51.560
Here are some examples.

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:53.870
And again, you don't have
to read through the details.

00:25:53.870 --> 00:25:56.950
But if you're curious, you can
take a look at the paper after.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:59.710
One interesting thing
here is that if you

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:03.100
look at the formal code, there's
actually some steps of reasoning

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:07.310
that the model is doing in the
informal thought, shown in gray,

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:11.590
that aren't really present
in the formal code.

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:15.460
And here's a longer
example, partly because it

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:18.490
looks impressive, but
also to just show you

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:20.810
that when you add in
these informal thoughts

00:26:20.810 --> 00:26:23.990
with the formal ones,
the output starts

00:26:23.990 --> 00:26:27.046
to get fairly non-trivial.

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:33.590
So after Lean-STaR, the
really exciting thing

00:26:33.590 --> 00:26:37.400
is that this idea of
augmenting models or reasoning

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:40.520
models with thoughts and
training them with reinforcement

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:46.070
learning has been adopted in
the theorem proving community.

00:26:46.070 --> 00:26:49.460
So in particular, very
rapidly, this idea

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:52.040
of incorporating
thoughts became a part

00:26:52.040 --> 00:26:53.810
of the state of the art systems.

00:26:53.810 --> 00:26:57.260
So here's the example that
I mentioned before called

00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:59.540
DeepSeek Prover,
where they incorporate

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:02.060
in the informal thoughts.

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:04.890
And then as I mentioned
just this morning,

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:11.180
there was the state-of-the-art
prover released by this startup.

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:13.760
And you can see that
they have this idea

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:20.760
of alternating between informal
reasoning and snippets of code.

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:23.790
And then more broadly,
these reasoning models

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:26.700
that generate long
chains of thought

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:29.130
and are trained with
reinforcement learning

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:35.080
have really begun to
revolutionize the LLM reasoning.

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:37.960
So I'm not going to go
into the details here.

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:41.910
But you have probably heard
of these models like OpenAI 01

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:43.990
and DeepSeek R1.

00:27:43.990 --> 00:27:47.140
And so it seems like,
at least currently,

00:27:47.140 --> 00:27:50.100
having this kind of
informal thinking

00:27:50.100 --> 00:27:55.230
is a really key part of
the reasoning procedure.

00:27:55.230 --> 00:27:59.880
So to recap, Lean-STaR
builds on this observation

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:03.240
that training only
on the formal code

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:06.930
may be insufficient to learn the
actual thought process that's

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:08.760
needed to produce the code.

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:12.660
And it gives a simple
algorithm for learning

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:15.780
to generate the thoughts.

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:19.120
Now I'll move from
informal thoughts

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:23.060
to informal provers
and informal proofs,

00:28:23.060 --> 00:28:26.260
and in particular, talk
about methods that prove

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:29.830
theorems by producing
this high-level sketch

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:32.040
and then filling in
the gaps of the sketch.

00:28:34.690 --> 00:28:38.470
So here, the motivation
is to combine

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:43.120
the benefits of higher level
reasoning and lower level

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:44.530
reasoning.

00:28:44.530 --> 00:28:48.160
And one motivation
for this is to think

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:50.140
about, how do we
actually write one

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:53.780
of these formal proofs in the
formal proof assistant-- sorry,

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:57.420
informal proofs in the
formal proof assistant.

00:28:57.420 --> 00:28:59.170
So again, you don't
have to worry too much

00:28:59.170 --> 00:29:01.280
about the details of this.

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:04.810
But here we're trying to prove
the statement on the left.

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:09.490
And what is notable here
is that we can somewhat

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:12.640
break this informal
proof up into these three

00:29:12.640 --> 00:29:15.670
different parts.

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:18.110
Then it turns out
that we can write

00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:20.720
this in the formal
system using something

00:29:20.720 --> 00:29:25.520
that is structured analogously
to the informal proof.

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:28.140
So this is a system
called Isabelle.

00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:30.530
And it has this
style where you can

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:36.260
write these intermediate
claims or conjectures.

00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:38.720
And if you look at
it, they somewhat

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:41.090
line up with the
different steps.

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:43.850
Here, you have 10
times 280 divided

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:46.430
by 40, which shows up here.

00:29:46.430 --> 00:29:49.250
And then this is a way
of basically writing,

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:52.610
OK, let's complete the proof.

00:29:52.610 --> 00:29:55.100
Now, the interesting thing
here is that you still

00:29:55.100 --> 00:30:00.350
need to produce a proof of one
of each one of these higher

00:30:00.350 --> 00:30:01.530
level steps.

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:03.560
So that is shown in green.

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:06.020
And so you can see here
that to actually come up

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:07.910
with that proof,
it doesn't really

00:30:07.910 --> 00:30:10.010
look like natural
language at all.

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:12.710
Here, it's actually
calling this SMT solver

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:16.080
and feeding it some facts.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:21.360
So interestingly, we have this
decomposition between high-level

00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:24.570
and these lower level things.

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:27.420
So let me actually say
a bit more about how

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:30.060
you produce these
lower level proofs

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:33.120
in a system like Isabelle.

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:36.570
So it turns out that there are
these very powerful and very

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:40.090
useful automation
tools called hammers,

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:42.210
or this one is
called Sledgehammer.

00:30:42.210 --> 00:30:44.610
And the basic idea
is that as you're

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:48.060
writing a proof as
a human, you could

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:52.110
write one of these
conjectures, and then you

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:54.570
could call the Sledgehammer.

00:30:54.570 --> 00:30:58.200
What it does under the
covers is it calls out

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:01.290
to external automated
provers, ones

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:04.350
that are based on first
order logic or higher order

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:07.260
logic or SMT solvers.

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:10.320
And these turn out to
be extremely effective

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:14.830
when you're using the
system for filling

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:18.040
in these little
gaps of the proof.

00:31:18.040 --> 00:31:20.710
What these are not
good at is, let's say

00:31:20.710 --> 00:31:23.590
you want to solve
an IMO problem,

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:29.440
then the Sledgehammer
methods just won't succeed.

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:33.020
The technical reason is that
based on how they're set up,

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:37.240
there is just such a large
search space of possible proofs.

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:40.300
And in particular, if you
think about this proof

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:43.400
as being structured as
these have statements,

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:45.370
they don't really
have the functionality

00:31:45.370 --> 00:31:48.400
to produce statements like that.

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:51.610
So in practice, these
are used interactively

00:31:51.610 --> 00:31:55.150
as you're writing
a proof as a human.

00:31:55.150 --> 00:31:57.790
So here, the idea is
we want to combine

00:31:57.790 --> 00:32:01.210
some kind of high-level
prover with a lower level

00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:03.790
prover like the Sledgehammer.

00:32:03.790 --> 00:32:07.930
And this leads to
our method, which

00:32:07.930 --> 00:32:10.180
is called
Draft-Sketch-Prove, which

00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:15.580
we presented in ICLR in 2023
and developed and released

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:19.210
in late 2022.

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:23.710
So here, the idea is to come up
with this three step procedure.

00:32:23.710 --> 00:32:27.140
First, we're going to
draft an informal proof.

00:32:27.140 --> 00:32:31.930
Then we're going to translate
it into this formal sketch.

00:32:31.930 --> 00:32:35.020
And then we're going to use
a low-level prover to fill

00:32:35.020 --> 00:32:37.720
in the gaps of the sketch.

00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:40.360
So to show you this
in more detail,

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:43.570
let's walk through
each one of these.

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:47.680
So first we start off
with an informal theorem

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:50.290
and a formal theorem statement.

00:32:50.290 --> 00:32:53.320
It's actually really important
to provide the system

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:55.310
with the formal
theorem statement

00:32:55.310 --> 00:32:58.360
because when we start
to translate things,

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:00.830
we want to make sure that
the model's not cheating,

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:05.230
and it's actually proving the
thing that we want it to prove.

00:33:05.230 --> 00:33:09.890
Then in the first step,
we have the system--

00:33:09.890 --> 00:33:13.230
so it could be a human
or it could be an LLM--

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:17.300
generate some informal proof.

00:33:17.300 --> 00:33:21.650
Then in the next step we have an
LLM translate the informal proof

00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:24.530
into what's called
a formal sketch.

00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:27.860
It's essentially what I showed
you before, except now, we

00:33:27.860 --> 00:33:31.370
leave these gaps to be filled,
which are the lower level

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:33.470
proofs.

00:33:33.470 --> 00:33:36.470
Then in the final stage,
we try to close the gap

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:38.370
with a lower level prover.

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:41.030
Here, we are actually
using the Sledgehammer tool

00:33:41.030 --> 00:33:42.920
that I told you about.

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:46.350
And if you're able to
close all of the gaps,

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:49.540
then you're guaranteed
to have a complete proof.

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:54.650
The interesting thing
is that this actually

00:33:54.650 --> 00:33:57.420
looks a lot different than
what I was showing you before,

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:00.540
where we have this
step-by-step proof search,

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:02.690
and maybe it's a tree search.

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:06.950
Now what we can do is we can
diversify the search space

00:34:06.950 --> 00:34:12.210
by just generating many possible
informal proofs or drafts,

00:34:12.210 --> 00:34:15.420
and/or generate many potential
translations of those

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:17.489
into formal sketches.

00:34:17.489 --> 00:34:19.750
And then we can just
try to fill in the gaps.

00:34:19.750 --> 00:34:23.560
And if the gaps are filled
in any of the sketches,

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:27.330
then we have a formal proof
that we could consider

00:34:27.330 --> 00:34:30.929
as the output of the system.

00:34:30.929 --> 00:34:34.469
So here's showing what
happens as we scale up

00:34:34.469 --> 00:34:37.050
the number of calls
to the pipeline.

00:34:37.050 --> 00:34:42.540
So here we're either generating
many informal proof drafts,

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:46.510
or in the case of using a
human written informal proof,

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:49.170
we're generating many
possible translations

00:34:49.170 --> 00:34:51.449
into the formal sketch.

00:34:51.449 --> 00:34:54.630
You can see on the left-hand
side that if we just call

00:34:54.630 --> 00:34:57.340
the system once,
then it actually--

00:34:57.340 --> 00:35:00.810
I mean, it proves some things,
but it's not very good.

00:35:00.810 --> 00:35:03.900
And we really see the benefits
as we scale up the amount

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:06.700
of proof search or sampling.

00:35:06.700 --> 00:35:10.790
And up here, we're proving
a large number of problems.

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:14.320
This is on the same
miniF2F data set.

00:35:14.320 --> 00:35:17.560
Now, this was in the days
of Codex and Minerva,

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:19.790
if you remember those models.

00:35:19.790 --> 00:35:21.970
And interestingly here,
if you look on the very

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:27.530
right-hand side, if we actually
sampled enough from the model,

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:30.050
then we were able
to, right at the end,

00:35:30.050 --> 00:35:34.180
surpass the performance of
using a human-written proof.

00:35:34.180 --> 00:35:36.530
And so this was
kind of exciting.

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:38.840
And probably, now that language
models are much better,

00:35:38.840 --> 00:35:45.910
we would be able to make
that transition even earlier.

00:35:45.910 --> 00:35:49.750
So here's an example of proving
an International Math Olympiad

00:35:49.750 --> 00:35:50.710
problem.

00:35:50.710 --> 00:35:54.400
So here the language model
produces the informal proof,

00:35:54.400 --> 00:35:55.840
shown in green.

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:59.020
Then the language model
produces the formal sketch,

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:00.550
shown in blue.

00:36:00.550 --> 00:36:03.490
You can see that here,
we actually, back then,

00:36:03.490 --> 00:36:07.820
even had these informal
thoughts in as comments.

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:12.680
Or here, it's showing the
informal version of the setup.

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:14.960
And then we have the
Sledgehammer system fill

00:36:14.960 --> 00:36:17.060
in the gaps, shown in purple.

00:36:17.060 --> 00:36:20.420
And you can see that sometimes
it's pretty non-trivial.

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:22.700
Here, again, it's
calling this SMT solver

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:26.440
and feeding in a bunch of
different facts or premises.

00:36:29.720 --> 00:36:36.570
So since then, I've put together
a tutorial for theorem proving.

00:36:36.570 --> 00:36:38.990
So it goes over the
step-by-step methods

00:36:38.990 --> 00:36:41.130
that I mentioned
in the first part,

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:45.230
and also has a notebook that
shows you how to implement

00:36:45.230 --> 00:36:47.750
this Draft-Sketch-Prove method.

00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:50.970
And in particular, it just calls
out to a language model API.

00:36:50.970 --> 00:36:55.820
So you can try it out with any
state-of-the-art model that you

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:58.690
want to, if you're
curious to learn more.

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:04.120
So to recap, I talked
about this method

00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:07.060
called Draft-Sketch-Prove,
which generates

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:10.780
these high-level sketches, and
then tries to fill in the gaps.

00:37:10.780 --> 00:37:15.250
And what this relied on is,
in this language, Isabelle,

00:37:15.250 --> 00:37:19.750
having this lower level
prover called Sledgehammer.

00:37:19.750 --> 00:37:24.730
So next, I'm going to talk about
our very recent work on trying

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:27.520
to build one of these
Sledgehammer provers

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:30.760
for Lean so that we
could more easily write

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:35.200
these sketch-based
proofs in Lean.

00:37:35.200 --> 00:37:40.850
So this is based on a paper
that is still not public,

00:37:40.850 --> 00:37:44.330
which is called Premise
Selection for a Lean Hammer,

00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:49.000
where we build a system
called Lean Hammer.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:51.190
So what is a hammer?

00:37:51.190 --> 00:37:54.890
So a hammer integrates,
as I mentioned,

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:56.980
these external provers.

00:37:56.980 --> 00:37:59.230
And there's a bit
of terminology here

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:01.840
that is a bit tricky
if you haven't

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:04.480
seen this distinction before.

00:38:04.480 --> 00:38:08.080
Specifically, what it does is it
integrates an automated theorem

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:12.250
prover into an interactive
theorem prover.

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:15.790
So an interactive theorem
prover is like the one

00:38:15.790 --> 00:38:17.530
I showed you, like Lean.

00:38:17.530 --> 00:38:21.430
And essentially, it's a
programming language that

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:24.130
is able to check the proofs.

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:27.010
However, the
language, on its own,

00:38:27.010 --> 00:38:30.040
doesn't give you a way of
automatically producing

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:30.920
the proofs.

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:34.720
You usually have to
write them as a human.

00:38:34.720 --> 00:38:38.140
An automated theorem
prover is something

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:41.080
that tries to
automatically find proofs.

00:38:41.080 --> 00:38:43.540
And traditionally,
these are based

00:38:43.540 --> 00:38:46.280
on different kinds of logics.

00:38:46.280 --> 00:38:50.320
So they might be based on higher
order logic provers or things

00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:53.710
like SMT solvers.

00:38:53.710 --> 00:38:58.510
And so as I mentioned before,
these automated theorem

00:38:58.510 --> 00:39:03.560
provers really struggle
with more complex proofs

00:39:03.560 --> 00:39:09.140
due to this large search
space in their formalism.

00:39:09.140 --> 00:39:13.430
So what we can do to try
to help with this problem

00:39:13.430 --> 00:39:16.580
is to solve this really
important problem, which

00:39:16.580 --> 00:39:19.010
is called premise selection.

00:39:19.010 --> 00:39:23.240
So basically, what you do is you
provide the automated theorem

00:39:23.240 --> 00:39:26.310
prover with many
different facts.

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:30.570
So you can provide it with
definitions, different theorems,

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:31.710
different lemmas.

00:39:31.710 --> 00:39:35.780
And then it goes off and
tries to prove the theorem

00:39:35.780 --> 00:39:37.550
that you want.

00:39:37.550 --> 00:39:40.280
And if we look at the
number of premises

00:39:40.280 --> 00:39:44.640
in, say, Lean's Mathlib,
then it's extremely large.

00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:48.350
So it's hundreds of
thousands of premises.

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:51.320
And so if you just give all of
those to the automated theorem

00:39:51.320 --> 00:39:57.500
prover, it will really
struggle because it has

00:39:57.500 --> 00:40:00.180
this really large search space.

00:40:00.180 --> 00:40:02.520
So the basic idea
of premise selection

00:40:02.520 --> 00:40:07.110
is to somehow cut down the
number of premises that we give

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:09.870
and only provide it ones
that are likely to be

00:40:09.870 --> 00:40:13.830
useful for proving the theorem.

00:40:13.830 --> 00:40:18.720
So then we can look at what is
the typical hammer pipeline.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:21.480
So here it is at a high level.

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:23.080
Let's say we're in Lean.

00:40:23.080 --> 00:40:27.720
Then we start off with the thing
we want to prove on the left.

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:30.840
Then we would pass it to some
kind of premise selection

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:32.460
system.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:36.060
Now, once we've selected
premises like definitions

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:39.360
and theorems, we have to
translate it from Lean

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:44.190
into the language of the
automated theorem prover.

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:45.690
Let's say that the
automated theorem

00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:48.180
prover finds a complete proof.

00:40:48.180 --> 00:40:50.970
Well, then we have to
actually translate it back

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:53.310
into a Lean proof.

00:40:53.310 --> 00:40:55.350
And so the way
that that's done is

00:40:55.350 --> 00:40:58.870
through the step, which is
called proof reconstruction.

00:40:58.870 --> 00:41:02.070
So it basically takes
the output and tries

00:41:02.070 --> 00:41:04.830
to produce a proof in Lean.

00:41:04.830 --> 00:41:08.925
Then as our output, we would see
this nice proof on the right.

00:41:08.925 --> 00:41:09.425
Sorry.

00:41:11.970 --> 00:41:17.070
So amazingly, there's been
a lot of really cool work

00:41:17.070 --> 00:41:21.150
in the community on
these different parts.

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:23.940
Usually, they're developed
somewhat independently

00:41:23.940 --> 00:41:28.860
of each other, but we have
this translation layer

00:41:28.860 --> 00:41:34.560
called Lean-auto that
can help translate

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:37.200
from Lean's
dependent type theory

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:40.230
into the higher order logic.

00:41:40.230 --> 00:41:41.970
An example automated
theorem prover

00:41:41.970 --> 00:41:44.550
is called zipper position.

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:47.320
And then for this
proof reconstruction,

00:41:47.320 --> 00:41:51.540
this was developed by Josh
Clune and collaborators,

00:41:51.540 --> 00:41:54.600
who's part of this
Lean hammer project.

00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:58.720
And there's a
system called Duper.

00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:03.010
So what we're interested in
here is two different things.

00:42:03.010 --> 00:42:07.300
First, we want to try to solve
this premise selection problem.

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:09.880
And then second,
we have to come up

00:42:09.880 --> 00:42:13.490
with some way of actually
combining these together.

00:42:13.490 --> 00:42:15.400
When I show it on
a slide it looks

00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:17.120
simple to put them together.

00:42:17.120 --> 00:42:20.330
But it's actually not clear
what the best way to do this is.

00:42:20.330 --> 00:42:25.510
And even more, we want to go
beyond the standard pipeline,

00:42:25.510 --> 00:42:28.420
which I'll talk about in a bit.

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:31.690
So first, let me talk about
the premise selection.

00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:34.960
What we do is to frame
this as retrieval

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:37.450
with a neural language model.

00:42:37.450 --> 00:42:42.730
So in particular, we have
a standard retrieval setup.

00:42:42.730 --> 00:42:44.840
Here, we embed our input.

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:47.470
So this will be the goal
that we want to prove.

00:42:47.470 --> 00:42:50.800
And we have a
language model that

00:42:50.800 --> 00:42:54.520
produces this embedding
shown on the right.

00:42:54.520 --> 00:42:58.230
Then we embed all of
our potential premises.

00:42:58.230 --> 00:43:02.100
So this will include
theorems, definitions, lemmas.

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:04.460
And they could be from Mathlib.

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:08.360
They could also be local
premises in your project

00:43:08.360 --> 00:43:13.310
or in the hypotheses that
you're going to prove.

00:43:13.310 --> 00:43:17.360
And with that, we get
this list of embeddings

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:19.520
shown on the right.

00:43:19.520 --> 00:43:24.530
Then to select the premises,
we take the dot product,

00:43:24.530 --> 00:43:29.630
use cosine similarity to
select the set of highest

00:43:29.630 --> 00:43:32.360
ranked premises.

00:43:32.360 --> 00:43:36.770
So here, we train with this
contrastive loss that is often

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:39.020
used to train retrieval models.

00:43:39.020 --> 00:43:43.820
So it boils down to collecting
these inputs, the states,

00:43:43.820 --> 00:43:48.530
as well as some positive
premises and negative premises.

00:43:48.530 --> 00:43:52.910
And it turns out that there's
actually a lot of nuance in how

00:43:52.910 --> 00:43:56.760
to do this properly, especially
when the output is going to be

00:43:56.760 --> 00:43:58.410
this Lean-auto--

00:43:58.410 --> 00:44:02.640
sorry, when the interface is
with this Lean-auto system

00:44:02.640 --> 00:44:05.280
in the hammer pipeline.

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:07.750
So I won't go into
all the details here.

00:44:07.750 --> 00:44:11.520
And when we release the paper,
it will have all the details.

00:44:11.520 --> 00:44:17.040
But even things like how and
whether you simplify terms,

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:20.400
how exactly you choose
these positive premises

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:22.740
and negative premises,
turn out to have

00:44:22.740 --> 00:44:27.420
a large impact on the
behavior of the retriever.

00:44:27.420 --> 00:44:30.450
And so we'll show in the
results in a second that

00:44:30.450 --> 00:44:33.600
based on some previous
retrievers that were trained,

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:37.260
our system that we trained does
a lot better when we put it

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:41.100
into this hammer pipeline.

00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:43.410
So then the question
is, how do we combine

00:44:43.410 --> 00:44:45.220
all of these different pieces?

00:44:45.220 --> 00:44:49.500
And so what we did is not
just have this linear picture

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:51.510
that I showed on
the previous slide,

00:44:51.510 --> 00:44:54.340
but we actually incorporate
these components

00:44:54.340 --> 00:44:56.500
into a tree search.

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:59.420
So there's a really
cool tree search method.

00:44:59.420 --> 00:45:04.940
And it's this symbolic
tree search called Aesop.

00:45:04.940 --> 00:45:09.190
It does this tree search over
different tactics or steps

00:45:09.190 --> 00:45:10.330
of the proof.

00:45:10.330 --> 00:45:13.090
And so what we do
is wire it up so

00:45:13.090 --> 00:45:16.210
that it can use the
selected premises

00:45:16.210 --> 00:45:19.750
to call different
tactics in Lean.

00:45:19.750 --> 00:45:24.070
And it can, at any time, query
this automated theorem prover

00:45:24.070 --> 00:45:26.230
using the selected premises.

00:45:26.230 --> 00:45:29.260
And so this step will
involve the translation

00:45:29.260 --> 00:45:34.810
that I mentioned, as well
as the proof reconstruction.

00:45:34.810 --> 00:45:37.210
So now with all of
these different parts

00:45:37.210 --> 00:45:41.020
that I've described,
you could say,

00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:44.380
how is this actually
useful as a user?

00:45:44.380 --> 00:45:50.380
Well, it turns out that we
can wrap this all into one

00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:51.800
simple command.

00:45:51.800 --> 00:45:55.490
So at any step of a
proof, you can just

00:45:55.490 --> 00:45:59.030
call this hammer
tactic, and then it'll

00:45:59.030 --> 00:46:01.670
run this tree search
that I mentioned,

00:46:01.670 --> 00:46:03.930
call the external
prover and all that.

00:46:03.930 --> 00:46:08.240
And then if it succeeds, it
will display the proof to you

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:11.150
on the right.

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:13.970
And what's cool about this
is you could potentially

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:17.000
enable this kind of
sketch-based proving

00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:19.490
that I was talking about before.

00:46:19.490 --> 00:46:23.990
So here, I want to show an
actual demo of the system

00:46:23.990 --> 00:46:29.130
where we took a human-written
proof sketch from this textbook,

00:46:29.130 --> 00:46:30.980
Mathematics and Lean.

00:46:30.980 --> 00:46:36.170
And we want to try to fill in
the gaps with the hammer system.

00:46:36.170 --> 00:46:39.260
So this theorem is
the irrationality

00:46:39.260 --> 00:46:42.440
of the square root of two.

00:46:42.440 --> 00:46:46.130
And yeah, sometimes
in the formal system,

00:46:46.130 --> 00:46:50.520
you'll write it in a
slightly different way.

00:46:50.520 --> 00:46:54.550
Yeah, the details are not
too important for the demo.

00:46:54.550 --> 00:46:59.010
But you can read through
after if you want.

00:46:59.010 --> 00:47:04.320
So here, we'll go
back into VS Code.

00:47:04.320 --> 00:47:09.510
And here is, first of
all, the simple theorem

00:47:09.510 --> 00:47:11.790
that I showed you before.

00:47:11.790 --> 00:47:18.150
And so what we can do is
we can call this hammer.

00:47:18.150 --> 00:47:24.270
And the first time you run
it should take a few seconds.

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:27.660
And so what it's done is
it's selected the premises

00:47:27.660 --> 00:47:33.750
and called the tree
search and all that.

00:47:33.750 --> 00:47:35.820
And now you can see
that it came back

00:47:35.820 --> 00:47:40.410
with this proof, this
theorem, or lemma.

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:44.130
So this one just says
that if two divides m

00:47:44.130 --> 00:47:48.100
squared then two will divide m.

00:47:48.100 --> 00:47:49.930
So it turns out that
this one will actually

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:55.030
be useful in this
irrationality proof below.

00:47:55.030 --> 00:47:57.670
So now we can go
step by step and try

00:47:57.670 --> 00:48:01.900
to fill in the gaps
with the hammer.

00:48:01.900 --> 00:48:04.330
So here it got this one.

00:48:04.330 --> 00:48:08.170
And we can just keep going.

00:48:08.170 --> 00:48:10.420
And interestingly,
up here, it actually

00:48:10.420 --> 00:48:13.010
used the Lemma that
we just proved.

00:48:13.010 --> 00:48:17.325
So you can see two divide
of two divide square.

00:48:20.080 --> 00:48:23.110
And so here it succeeded again.

00:48:23.110 --> 00:48:24.840
And I'll just keep
going for fun.

00:48:33.910 --> 00:48:37.732
So the idea here is
that by doing this,

00:48:37.732 --> 00:48:39.190
you don't necessarily
have to worry

00:48:39.190 --> 00:48:41.630
about these different details.

00:48:41.630 --> 00:48:43.540
So here's a kind
of interesting one

00:48:43.540 --> 00:48:47.513
where it's actually calling
the automated theorem prover.

00:48:47.513 --> 00:48:49.430
So you can see all of
the different facts that

00:48:49.430 --> 00:48:50.840
are being passed to it.

00:48:50.840 --> 00:48:58.310
And here, we could see the
call, the actual call to Duper.

00:48:58.310 --> 00:49:01.550
So Duper was this proof
reconstruction method

00:49:01.550 --> 00:49:04.250
that I mentioned before.

00:49:04.250 --> 00:49:06.750
So if you want, you could
treat this as the proof,

00:49:06.750 --> 00:49:08.680
or you can just leave
this one in there.

00:49:11.330 --> 00:49:14.900
For this step,
turns out you have

00:49:14.900 --> 00:49:19.490
to do this one step of
clearing out this hypothesis.

00:49:19.490 --> 00:49:22.790
But then you can
call the hammer.

00:49:22.790 --> 00:49:27.560
And just for fun, I'll
finish off this proof just

00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:30.865
to show you that it's possible.

00:49:42.560 --> 00:49:44.400
And then here's the last step.

00:49:44.400 --> 00:49:45.535
And we can call the hammer.

00:49:49.220 --> 00:49:51.630
OK, so now we have a complete
proof of the theorem.

00:49:51.630 --> 00:49:54.270
And we started off with
these high-level steps

00:49:54.270 --> 00:49:59.870
and were able to
fill in the gaps.

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:03.100
So again, here's the summary.

00:50:05.750 --> 00:50:08.630
Then what we could
do is evaluate this

00:50:08.630 --> 00:50:12.380
with different premise
selectors swapped in.

00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:16.250
So here's some
results on theorems

00:50:16.250 --> 00:50:21.110
that we held out from Mathlib
as a test set And we just

00:50:21.110 --> 00:50:24.470
run the hammer at the
first step of the proof.

00:50:24.470 --> 00:50:26.180
So on the left here,
you can see what

00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:30.980
happens if you don't have
the premise selection.

00:50:30.980 --> 00:50:36.990
Then here is using a retriever
that was from prior work.

00:50:36.990 --> 00:50:42.560
What we did is we retrained
it on the exact same snapshot

00:50:42.560 --> 00:50:46.410
of Mathlib so it
had up-to-date data.

00:50:46.410 --> 00:50:50.040
And it's partially showing that
the specific decisions that we

00:50:50.040 --> 00:50:53.430
make in training our retriever
were really important

00:50:53.430 --> 00:50:55.890
for performance.

00:50:55.890 --> 00:50:58.980
Interestingly, there's a
symbolic premise selection

00:50:58.980 --> 00:51:00.000
system.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:02.340
I believe it's based
on the one that

00:51:02.340 --> 00:51:05.280
was in Isabelle Sledgehammer.

00:51:05.280 --> 00:51:08.940
So Kim Morrison from
the Lean community

00:51:08.940 --> 00:51:10.990
re-implemented it in Lean.

00:51:10.990 --> 00:51:15.360
And you can see that it actually
does pretty decently when

00:51:15.360 --> 00:51:17.820
it's in our hammer pipeline.

00:51:17.820 --> 00:51:20.550
Here is the Lean
hammer retriever

00:51:20.550 --> 00:51:24.240
as the fourth bar with
the diagonal lines.

00:51:24.240 --> 00:51:25.950
And then interestingly,
it ends up

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:30.820
being complementary to the
symbolic MePO retriever.

00:51:30.820 --> 00:51:34.020
So you can see with
these crosshatched lines,

00:51:34.020 --> 00:51:36.240
you get a performance
boost if you just

00:51:36.240 --> 00:51:39.180
take the union of premises.

00:51:39.180 --> 00:51:42.590
And then in absolute terms, it's
starting to get pretty good.

00:51:42.590 --> 00:51:45.700
So all the way on the
right is the performance

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:50.950
that we get if we just use
the ground truth premises that

00:51:50.950 --> 00:51:53.290
occur in the human proof.

00:51:53.290 --> 00:51:54.790
And you can see
that the performance

00:51:54.790 --> 00:51:58.330
is starting to approach
the performance that we

00:51:58.330 --> 00:51:59.340
get if we use those.

00:52:02.620 --> 00:52:06.190
So to recap, I talked about
two different approaches

00:52:06.190 --> 00:52:11.680
for combining informal
and formal provers or even

00:52:11.680 --> 00:52:14.260
formal/informal proofs.

00:52:14.260 --> 00:52:16.220
I talked about
Draft-Sketch-Prove,

00:52:16.220 --> 00:52:19.990
which involves drafting
an informal proof,

00:52:19.990 --> 00:52:22.900
generating a formal
sketch, and then filling

00:52:22.900 --> 00:52:25.120
in the low-level details.

00:52:25.120 --> 00:52:27.640
And I talked about
Lean hammer, which

00:52:27.640 --> 00:52:32.500
is trying to bring this paradigm
to Lean or make it easier.

00:52:32.500 --> 00:52:36.160
So in particular, we try
to build this hammer.

00:52:36.160 --> 00:52:40.100
And it involves this neural
premise selection and a tree

00:52:40.100 --> 00:52:42.880
search and calling out to
the automated theorem prover.

00:52:45.650 --> 00:52:49.910
And one interesting
takeaway from this is--

00:52:49.910 --> 00:52:53.340
I didn't go into the detail
of the model that we trained--

00:52:53.340 --> 00:52:57.290
but the largest model that we
trained was just a 100 million

00:52:57.290 --> 00:53:00.540
parameter retriever,
so very small.

00:53:00.540 --> 00:53:02.490
And as you maybe
saw in the demo,

00:53:02.490 --> 00:53:06.050
you can get some pretty
non-trivial behavior

00:53:06.050 --> 00:53:08.870
or performance from
integrating it in.

00:53:08.870 --> 00:53:12.240
So it's kind of exciting
because in these cases,

00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:14.970
even these fairly small,
by today's standards,

00:53:14.970 --> 00:53:20.030
neural networks can
be fairly powerful.

00:53:20.030 --> 00:53:22.310
So in the last
part of the talk, I

00:53:22.310 --> 00:53:27.950
want to talk about trying to
use AI or techniques that I've

00:53:27.950 --> 00:53:32.580
talked about to actually assist
in research-level mathematics.

00:53:32.580 --> 00:53:36.860
So not just prove
the IMO problems

00:53:36.860 --> 00:53:41.860
but try to help out in
these more complex projects

00:53:41.860 --> 00:53:47.530
that mathematicians, or
if you're a Lean hobbyist,

00:53:47.530 --> 00:53:52.630
or interested in formalizing
something difficult.

00:53:52.630 --> 00:53:55.570
So what does it
actually look like to

00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:58.240
formalize research-level math?

00:53:58.240 --> 00:54:00.370
Well, here we can look
at this blog post that

00:54:00.370 --> 00:54:02.650
was written by Terence Tao.

00:54:02.650 --> 00:54:05.110
And he breaks down
the steps that

00:54:05.110 --> 00:54:10.390
were involved in formalizing
a proof from this paper, which

00:54:10.390 --> 00:54:12.520
is shown on the left.

00:54:12.520 --> 00:54:15.400
So again, the goal was to
take one of the main theorems

00:54:15.400 --> 00:54:18.250
from the paper and then
try to write it in Lean

00:54:18.250 --> 00:54:21.100
and come up with a formal proof.

00:54:21.100 --> 00:54:26.020
And the first step is to write
what is called a blueprint.

00:54:26.020 --> 00:54:29.830
So here you go step by
step and think about

00:54:29.830 --> 00:54:35.360
how would this proof decompose
into different definitions,

00:54:35.360 --> 00:54:37.100
into different lemmas.

00:54:37.100 --> 00:54:41.800
And ultimately, we can come up
with the kind of final theorem

00:54:41.800 --> 00:54:44.470
and proof that we care about.

00:54:44.470 --> 00:54:46.880
And as you can see
here, on the top,

00:54:46.880 --> 00:54:50.950
it's showing a dependency
graph of all of these theorems

00:54:50.950 --> 00:54:52.750
and all of these definitions.

00:54:52.750 --> 00:54:55.520
So it gets fairly complex.

00:54:55.520 --> 00:54:58.570
And at the very bottom,
you have the final theorem

00:54:58.570 --> 00:55:00.970
and final proof.

00:55:00.970 --> 00:55:03.130
And so, interestingly
here, everything

00:55:03.130 --> 00:55:07.730
is still written in
informal language.

00:55:07.730 --> 00:55:11.170
So you could see here
the theorem and the proof

00:55:11.170 --> 00:55:13.540
just written in LaTeX.

00:55:13.540 --> 00:55:15.710
And then you try
to think about, OK,

00:55:15.710 --> 00:55:20.140
how do we actually
realize this in Lean.

00:55:20.140 --> 00:55:23.140
And so what it comes
down to is a large set

00:55:23.140 --> 00:55:26.650
of these formal definitions
and formal lemmas

00:55:26.650 --> 00:55:28.400
and formal theorems.

00:55:28.400 --> 00:55:32.440
And then ultimately, you're
able to state your final theorem

00:55:32.440 --> 00:55:35.600
and write the proof of it.

00:55:35.600 --> 00:55:39.350
So at the end, you have
this proof and this theorem

00:55:39.350 --> 00:55:42.680
that are really dependent
on this graph of things

00:55:42.680 --> 00:55:45.020
that I showed you.

00:55:45.020 --> 00:55:51.860
So a question is, at what point
of this procedure could AI help?

00:55:51.860 --> 00:55:55.520
So maybe, at least
for now, we don't

00:55:55.520 --> 00:55:58.440
have it do this whole
pipeline end to end.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:01.550
But we could think about,
what is the simplest

00:56:01.550 --> 00:56:07.850
thing that we could have it do
and that we could have trust.

00:56:07.850 --> 00:56:11.390
So one thing you could do is
you could look at this blueprint

00:56:11.390 --> 00:56:15.620
and look at just one of
these theorems or lemmas that

00:56:15.620 --> 00:56:21.890
is necessary for the proof,
but is just one part of it.

00:56:21.890 --> 00:56:25.250
So here's an example where
we have this lemma, which

00:56:25.250 --> 00:56:26.960
occurs somewhere in here.

00:56:26.960 --> 00:56:29.660
And if we give this
to a language model

00:56:29.660 --> 00:56:32.270
and it produces a
proof, then, again, we

00:56:32.270 --> 00:56:35.680
can trust the output
because Lean has checked it.

00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:38.380
And in some sense, it's
helped the development,

00:56:38.380 --> 00:56:41.610
because then the human
proof writer doesn't

00:56:41.610 --> 00:56:45.630
have to go through this step.

00:56:45.630 --> 00:56:48.690
So in order to do
this, we really

00:56:48.690 --> 00:56:56.610
have to overcome at least two
different gaps that occur.

00:56:56.610 --> 00:56:59.940
The first I'll call
the accessibility gap.

00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:04.500
And this is just the simple
observation that a lot of times

00:57:04.500 --> 00:57:07.110
these methods are
developed, but then they're

00:57:07.110 --> 00:57:10.230
actually hard to integrate
into actual tools.

00:57:10.230 --> 00:57:14.740
So for example, the system
might be very capable,

00:57:14.740 --> 00:57:17.340
but it might not be open source.

00:57:17.340 --> 00:57:20.410
Or the results might
be very impressive,

00:57:20.410 --> 00:57:24.180
but then they're actually very
expensive or costly to run.

00:57:24.180 --> 00:57:26.860
And so when you think about
this practical setting,

00:57:26.860 --> 00:57:29.400
it adds constraints
onto the methods

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:32.220
that you have to develop.

00:57:32.220 --> 00:57:36.490
But luckily, we actually do have
some tools already available

00:57:36.490 --> 00:57:42.100
that could potentially be
useful for these simple gaps.

00:57:42.100 --> 00:57:45.910
So we developed one of
them called LLM Lean.

00:57:45.910 --> 00:57:50.260
You could go download it and
use it right now if you want.

00:57:50.260 --> 00:57:52.960
So the basic idea
is to just create

00:57:52.960 --> 00:57:57.850
this interface between Lean
and some language model.

00:57:57.850 --> 00:58:00.970
So what it does is it gives
you these different commands

00:58:00.970 --> 00:58:05.050
or tactics, like, this one
queries a language model

00:58:05.050 --> 00:58:08.560
to try to produce the
next step of the proof.

00:58:08.560 --> 00:58:12.170
And then we can actually check
the outputs of the model.

00:58:12.170 --> 00:58:15.280
So we could discard the
incorrect ones and just

00:58:15.280 --> 00:58:18.320
display ones that
finish the proof.

00:58:18.320 --> 00:58:19.750
Those are shown in green.

00:58:19.750 --> 00:58:23.510
Or blue ones are ones
that don't give an error,

00:58:23.510 --> 00:58:27.220
but don't necessarily
finish the proof.

00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:31.820
And in particular, you
can, even nowadays,

00:58:31.820 --> 00:58:34.110
have a model running
on your laptop.

00:58:34.110 --> 00:58:35.750
So there's these
different libraries

00:58:35.750 --> 00:58:40.320
that have been developed
like Ollama or LM Studio.

00:58:40.320 --> 00:58:43.460
And you could basically host a
language model on your laptop,

00:58:43.460 --> 00:58:47.030
and then use LLM Lean
to communicate with it.

00:58:47.030 --> 00:58:51.920
Similarly, you can have it
communicate with some API.

00:58:51.920 --> 00:58:57.710
So it could be OpenAI, it could
be Claude, Gemini, together.ai.

00:58:57.710 --> 00:58:59.690
And so now you can
actually try to use

00:58:59.690 --> 00:59:02.240
these models to
give you suggestions

00:59:02.240 --> 00:59:05.240
that are checked in Lean.

00:59:05.240 --> 00:59:12.320
So as the last demo, I
wanted to just show you this.

00:59:12.320 --> 00:59:17.600
And so let's look
for the other window.

00:59:17.600 --> 00:59:21.020
So let's go back to this
really simple theorem

00:59:21.020 --> 00:59:24.410
that I showed at the
beginning of the talk.

00:59:24.410 --> 00:59:29.420
And you might have noticed that
I imported this LLM Lean here.

00:59:29.420 --> 00:59:35.690
And so now we could call this
LLM step command that I showed.

00:59:35.690 --> 00:59:39.080
And actually, it comes up
with this intro command

00:59:39.080 --> 00:59:42.830
that I showed you at the
beginning of the talk.

00:59:42.830 --> 00:59:46.460
Then we could call it again.

00:59:46.460 --> 00:59:50.470
And in this case, it was
able to finish the proof.

00:59:53.420 --> 00:59:57.320
So another thing
you can do is you

00:59:57.320 --> 01:00:01.140
could say, try to just
do this exact command,

01:00:01.140 --> 01:00:03.500
so try to prove it in one step.

01:00:03.500 --> 01:00:11.390
It actually came up with this
bit more clever, one-step proof.

01:00:11.390 --> 01:00:12.950
There's another
command that just

01:00:12.950 --> 01:00:17.870
tries to prove it in
end-to-end, like, fully

01:00:17.870 --> 01:00:22.850
prove it instead of just
predicting the next step.

01:00:22.850 --> 01:00:29.880
So here, the model comes up
with multiple potential proofs.

01:00:29.880 --> 01:00:33.060
And you can see they're a
bit different style, maybe,

01:00:33.060 --> 01:00:35.340
a bit longer.

01:00:35.340 --> 01:00:43.398
So you can also do other fun
things like shorten this proof,

01:00:43.398 --> 01:00:48.420
then put it as a comment
and then call llmqed.

01:00:51.300 --> 01:00:52.810
So let's see what it does.

01:00:52.810 --> 01:00:57.450
And yeah, so now it gives us
some options that are shorter.

01:00:57.450 --> 01:00:58.980
Actually, this is pretty short.

01:00:58.980 --> 01:01:01.475
You could just prove
it in one call.

01:01:04.260 --> 01:01:06.810
So yeah, this is a tool
that you could use.

01:01:06.810 --> 01:01:12.210
Today, you just need to
have a language model API.

01:01:12.210 --> 01:01:16.895
And then you can import
it into a Lean project.

01:01:20.520 --> 01:01:24.930
So interestingly,
let's see here,

01:01:24.930 --> 01:01:27.560
the methods are still
bottlenecked, obviously,

01:01:27.560 --> 01:01:32.380
by how good the model
is at proving theorems.

01:01:32.380 --> 01:01:37.840
But even now, I went through
Terence Tao's project, this PFR

01:01:37.840 --> 01:01:41.470
project, and just picked a file.

01:01:41.470 --> 01:01:43.600
Yeah, this one was
actually able to fill

01:01:43.600 --> 01:01:48.812
in some of these small lemmas.

01:01:48.812 --> 01:01:50.770
This was actually the
example that I showed you

01:01:50.770 --> 01:01:55.270
before on the previous slide.

01:01:55.270 --> 01:02:01.090
So another thing is, I mentioned
that the models right now are

01:02:01.090 --> 01:02:02.180
the bottleneck.

01:02:02.180 --> 01:02:05.000
We have the interface set up.

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:08.770
And so now it just comes
down to whether the model can

01:02:08.770 --> 01:02:14.650
complete theorems that occur
in these kinds of projects.

01:02:14.650 --> 01:02:20.200
So this leads to a second gap,
which is the gap in the way

01:02:20.200 --> 01:02:23.950
that we benchmark these
theorem proving models.

01:02:23.950 --> 01:02:29.030
So largely, these models are
benchmarked on their ability

01:02:29.030 --> 01:02:31.890
to prove these math
competition problems,

01:02:31.890 --> 01:02:34.190
like, ones from the IMO.

01:02:34.190 --> 01:02:39.680
And what these problems have is
they're fairly self-contained.

01:02:39.680 --> 01:02:41.720
So you can usually
state the problem

01:02:41.720 --> 01:02:44.180
in just one line of code.

01:02:44.180 --> 01:02:50.480
And then they're designed to
somewhat use standard results.

01:02:50.480 --> 01:02:53.310
So there's a standard set.

01:02:53.310 --> 01:02:57.030
And a lot of them have been
implemented in Mathlib.

01:02:57.030 --> 01:03:00.960
And then constructing the
proof is very difficult.

01:03:00.960 --> 01:03:02.850
It requires a lot of creativity.

01:03:02.850 --> 01:03:08.270
But it's relying on
these standard results.

01:03:08.270 --> 01:03:10.220
If we compare that
to this project

01:03:10.220 --> 01:03:12.080
that I've been
telling you about,

01:03:12.080 --> 01:03:14.960
these have these
complex dependencies

01:03:14.960 --> 01:03:17.750
from throughout the project.

01:03:17.750 --> 01:03:23.490
So in particular, this theorem,
to even state it or to prove it,

01:03:23.490 --> 01:03:25.800
it might actually
rely on these brand

01:03:25.800 --> 01:03:29.070
new definitions and brand
new lemmas that have

01:03:29.070 --> 01:03:31.530
been created for the project.

01:03:31.530 --> 01:03:34.410
And so unless we
continually train the model

01:03:34.410 --> 01:03:37.440
on all of the current
projects, it really

01:03:37.440 --> 01:03:42.120
has to learn to prove theorems
in this context-dependent

01:03:42.120 --> 01:03:46.290
setting that I'm
showing on the right.

01:03:46.290 --> 01:03:50.520
So this leads to our
new benchmark called

01:03:50.520 --> 01:03:53.470
miniCTX, or mini context.

01:03:53.470 --> 01:03:56.520
And we'll also be
presenting this at ICLR

01:03:56.520 --> 01:04:00.210
as an oral presentation.

01:04:00.210 --> 01:04:04.500
So here what we do is we made
this observation that I just

01:04:04.500 --> 01:04:10.200
told you about that when we
look at these research-level or

01:04:10.200 --> 01:04:13.680
actual formalizations
that people do in Lean,

01:04:13.680 --> 01:04:16.890
they are really in this
context-dependent setting

01:04:16.890 --> 01:04:19.260
where you want to
prove a theorem,

01:04:19.260 --> 01:04:23.000
and it depends on a
whole repository of code,

01:04:23.000 --> 01:04:27.610
new definitions, or
new auxiliary lemmas.

01:04:27.610 --> 01:04:31.240
So the idea of the
benchmark is to test models

01:04:31.240 --> 01:04:34.150
on actual real Lean projects.

01:04:34.150 --> 01:04:38.000
So in particular, we
choose some date cutoff.

01:04:38.000 --> 01:04:40.700
And then we look at a
project like Mathlib.

01:04:40.700 --> 01:04:42.610
And we can take all
of the theorems that

01:04:42.610 --> 01:04:45.370
were developed after that date.

01:04:45.370 --> 01:04:48.880
Similarly, we can look at
when a project was created

01:04:48.880 --> 01:04:55.330
and take theorems and contexts
from those different projects.

01:04:55.330 --> 01:04:59.560
So this PFR project by
Terence Tao and collaborators

01:04:59.560 --> 01:05:01.480
that I've been
telling you about is

01:05:01.480 --> 01:05:03.970
one of the projects that
was in the first version

01:05:03.970 --> 01:05:06.970
of this benchmark.

01:05:06.970 --> 01:05:10.000
Then the idea is
we also make tools

01:05:10.000 --> 01:05:13.720
so that you can automatically
extract benchmark problems

01:05:13.720 --> 01:05:15.610
from these projects.

01:05:15.610 --> 01:05:18.580
So what you can do is
update the benchmark

01:05:18.580 --> 01:05:22.820
to stay ahead of the training
cutoffs for these language

01:05:22.820 --> 01:05:23.870
models.

01:05:23.870 --> 01:05:28.680
So we've actually already
released a miniCTX version 2.

01:05:28.680 --> 01:05:32.480
And it has more
recent Lean projects

01:05:32.480 --> 01:05:37.370
that have been developed in
the past couple of months.

01:05:37.370 --> 01:05:41.700
So here, we have two
different types of contexts.

01:05:41.700 --> 01:05:45.350
And these are basically
what dependencies

01:05:45.350 --> 01:05:48.020
are needed to prove a theorem.

01:05:48.020 --> 01:05:53.300
And what we do is we build
tooling to automatically extract

01:05:53.300 --> 01:05:55.400
things like dependencies.

01:05:55.400 --> 01:05:59.390
And we can look
at, does a theorem

01:05:59.390 --> 01:06:02.210
depend on things just
in the current file

01:06:02.210 --> 01:06:06.350
or does it depend on things
across multiple files.

01:06:06.350 --> 01:06:08.900
And then we can also
get things like,

01:06:08.900 --> 01:06:12.380
what are the set of
available premises

01:06:12.380 --> 01:06:15.140
when you're going to
prove this theorem, so

01:06:15.140 --> 01:06:17.510
a very realistic setting.

01:06:17.510 --> 01:06:20.830
So in particular, let's look
at two different settings.

01:06:20.830 --> 01:06:23.940
One setting is where
all of the dependencies

01:06:23.940 --> 01:06:27.780
just appear in the current
file, and then the other

01:06:27.780 --> 01:06:33.300
is when the dependencies
can span multiple files.

01:06:33.300 --> 01:06:36.660
So the first thing we want
to do is actually demonstrate

01:06:36.660 --> 01:06:39.510
that this context
actually matters.

01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:43.500
So, again, I've told you
about this really common way

01:06:43.500 --> 01:06:46.350
of training theorem
provers, which

01:06:46.350 --> 01:06:48.990
is by just giving
them the proof state

01:06:48.990 --> 01:06:52.030
and then having them
produce a next step,

01:06:52.030 --> 01:06:55.620
or having them produce
thoughts if you want.

01:06:55.620 --> 01:07:01.290
So here what we did is develop
this simple baseline method,

01:07:01.290 --> 01:07:03.660
which additionally
gives the model all

01:07:03.660 --> 01:07:07.200
of the code in the file so far.

01:07:07.200 --> 01:07:11.200
So this can include new
definitions, new lemmas,

01:07:11.200 --> 01:07:14.790
even new notation, or
natural language comments.

01:07:14.790 --> 01:07:18.100
And we want to see how this
compared to just taking

01:07:18.100 --> 01:07:21.250
in the proof state.

01:07:21.250 --> 01:07:25.150
So interestingly, if you look at
a competition problem data set,

01:07:25.150 --> 01:07:29.240
like, miniF2F, it actually
really doesn't matter.

01:07:29.240 --> 01:07:32.170
You can do, pretty
similarly, whether you

01:07:32.170 --> 01:07:34.600
take in additional
context, it would just

01:07:34.600 --> 01:07:37.930
be the import
statements for miniF2F,

01:07:37.930 --> 01:07:40.150
or if you just take
the proof state.

01:07:40.150 --> 01:07:43.510
So this might be one reason why
a lot of these state-of-the-art

01:07:43.510 --> 01:07:49.270
theorem proving methods don't
really deal with or support any

01:07:49.270 --> 01:07:51.250
external context.

01:07:51.250 --> 01:07:55.670
However, when we evaluate
them on our benchmark,

01:07:55.670 --> 01:08:00.170
miniCTX, which has the theorems
from real-world projects,

01:08:00.170 --> 01:08:03.700
we can see this dramatic
gap in the performance.

01:08:03.700 --> 01:08:07.820
And the reason is pretty obvious
if you just look at a Lean file,

01:08:07.820 --> 01:08:10.280
it's because in
these real projects,

01:08:10.280 --> 01:08:14.530
you're defining these new
definitions and new lemmas

01:08:14.530 --> 01:08:17.290
like I discussed.

01:08:17.290 --> 01:08:22.510
Interestingly, you can also
do this premise selection

01:08:22.510 --> 01:08:26.680
and give the language
model the premises.

01:08:26.680 --> 01:08:31.450
And you can see variations based
on whether the theorem requires

01:08:31.450 --> 01:08:36.220
just in-file information
or auto-file information.

01:08:36.220 --> 01:08:38.649
So all the way on the
left, we have theorems

01:08:38.649 --> 01:08:41.950
with very low dependencies.

01:08:41.950 --> 01:08:45.550
The first set of bar graphs
shows high dependencies

01:08:45.550 --> 01:08:47.330
on in-file premises.

01:08:47.330 --> 01:08:50.350
And so here you could see
that you do pretty well

01:08:50.350 --> 01:08:52.870
by just giving the
language model all

01:08:52.870 --> 01:08:54.850
the code in the current file.

01:08:54.850 --> 01:08:56.920
And then in the third
set of bar graphs,

01:08:56.920 --> 01:08:58.810
you see what
happens when we have

01:08:58.810 --> 01:09:00.710
these cross-file dependencies.

01:09:00.710 --> 01:09:04.149
And you can see that adding
in this premise selection

01:09:04.149 --> 01:09:06.939
leads to some improvement.

01:09:06.939 --> 01:09:11.810
So here, we're using an older
premise selector system.

01:09:11.810 --> 01:09:15.680
So we're excited about
integrating our Lean hammer

01:09:15.680 --> 01:09:20.029
into this for addressing
the cross-file dependencies.

01:09:20.029 --> 01:09:22.880
And in general,
there's a wide space

01:09:22.880 --> 01:09:26.634
of methods that could be
developed for this setting.

01:09:29.569 --> 01:09:34.640
So I should also mention that we
provide the model with this file

01:09:34.640 --> 01:09:35.220
tuning.

01:09:35.220 --> 01:09:39.779
So it's trained to take in the
file context through Ollama.

01:09:39.779 --> 01:09:42.380
So you can download it,
run it on your laptop,

01:09:42.380 --> 01:09:45.649
and use it inside of Lean.

01:09:45.649 --> 01:09:47.840
And then all of the
methods that are

01:09:47.840 --> 01:09:50.689
in LLM Lean that are calling
the proprietary language

01:09:50.689 --> 01:09:53.990
models, or the API-based
ones, they'll actually

01:09:53.990 --> 01:09:57.355
prompt the model with all of
the code in the current file.

01:10:00.140 --> 01:10:03.110
And then as I mentioned,
everything from the project

01:10:03.110 --> 01:10:04.170
is open source.

01:10:04.170 --> 01:10:08.660
So the data, the models,
our data extraction tool,

01:10:08.660 --> 01:10:12.560
and even the
evaluation code, which

01:10:12.560 --> 01:10:16.300
is like a wrapper
of the Lean ripple.

01:10:16.300 --> 01:10:19.170
And so you can check out
those resources online.

01:10:22.210 --> 01:10:24.110
So as I mentioned
in the third part,

01:10:24.110 --> 01:10:28.510
if we look at these
real Lean projects

01:10:28.510 --> 01:10:31.810
or what it would take to
formalize research-level math,

01:10:31.810 --> 01:10:34.960
it opens up some
unique challenges.

01:10:34.960 --> 01:10:37.310
These aren't the only
challenges, of course,

01:10:37.310 --> 01:10:41.170
but two that are really
present in the kind of research

01:10:41.170 --> 01:10:42.550
community right now.

01:10:42.550 --> 01:10:47.650
One is that we still have a
gap between the AI advances

01:10:47.650 --> 01:10:50.440
and tools that people can use.

01:10:50.440 --> 01:10:53.410
And then second, the
benchmarks don't always

01:10:53.410 --> 01:10:55.960
reflect all of the
capabilities that

01:10:55.960 --> 01:11:01.120
are required to do this
real-world formalization.

01:11:01.120 --> 01:11:04.730
And so this is very much
an open research direction.

01:11:04.730 --> 01:11:10.930
And as I mentioned, this
benchmark is the first step.

01:11:10.930 --> 01:11:14.140
So in summary, in this
talk, I talked about three

01:11:14.140 --> 01:11:17.650
different ways to try to
bridge informal and formal

01:11:17.650 --> 01:11:19.270
mathematical reasoning.

01:11:19.270 --> 01:11:23.080
I talked about incorporating
informal thoughts by training

01:11:23.080 --> 01:11:25.540
models to think informally.

01:11:25.540 --> 01:11:28.180
I talked about incorporating
informal provers

01:11:28.180 --> 01:11:33.490
or informal proofs through
methods like sketch proofs,

01:11:33.490 --> 01:11:35.373
and try to fill in the gaps.

01:11:35.373 --> 01:11:36.790
And then towards
the end, I talked

01:11:36.790 --> 01:11:43.480
about trying to take, say, a
research paper and look at what

01:11:43.480 --> 01:11:46.510
is involved in producing
a formal version of it

01:11:46.510 --> 01:11:49.330
and looking at what are
some initial ways where

01:11:49.330 --> 01:11:52.540
AI can help in the process.

01:11:52.540 --> 01:11:56.620
And so with that, that is
all I have for today's talk.

01:11:56.620 --> 01:12:00.070
And I'd like to thank all
of the amazing collaborators

01:12:00.070 --> 01:12:02.290
on these various works.

01:12:02.290 --> 01:12:04.030
They're listed here.

01:12:04.030 --> 01:12:07.120
And again, my name is Sean.

01:12:07.120 --> 01:12:09.380
And I have my
email address here,

01:12:09.380 --> 01:12:12.660
so please feel
free to reach out.

